2) Required API Endpoints

GET /tickets

Description:
Retrieve all support tickets.

This endpoint should also support optional query parameters
for filtering tickets.

Examples:

/tickets?status=open
/tickets?priority=urgent
/tickets?category=payment
/tickets?customer_name=Ali

Multiple filters should also work together:

/tickets?category=payment&priority=urgent&status=open


GET /tickets/{ticket_id}

Description:
Retrieve a single support ticket using its unique ticket ID.

If the ticket does not exist, the API should return an
appropriate 404 error response.


POST /tickets

Description:
Create a new customer support ticket.

The request should contain the customer's information and
ticket details.

The API should automatically generate:

- Ticket ID
- Ticket creation time
- Initial ticket status

The client should not provide these automatically generated
values.


GET /tickets/statistics

Description:
Return a summary of the support tickets.

The response should include information such as:

- Total number of tickets
- Number of open tickets
- Number of in-progress tickets
- Number of resolved tickets
- Number of closed tickets
- Number of urgent tickets
- Number of high-priority tickets

All values must be calculated dynamically from tickets.json.


GET /tickets/statistics/categories

Description:
Return the number of tickets for each ticket category.

For example:

{
    "authentication": 5,
    "payment": 8,
    "technical": 10,
    "account": 4,
    "billing": 6,
    "other": 2
}

These values must be calculated from the existing tickets.


GET /tickets/high-priority

Description:
Retrieve all high-priority support tickets.

This endpoint should return tickets where the priority is:

- high
- urgent


GET /customers/{customer_name}/tickets

Description:
Retrieve all support tickets belonging to a specific customer.

For example:

/customers/Ali Khan/tickets

should return all tickets created by Ali Khan.

If the customer has no tickets, return an empty list.


3) GET /tickets — Query Parameters

The /tickets endpoint must support optional query parameters.

Supported filters:

- customer_name
- status
- priority
- category

Examples:

/tickets?status=open

Retrieve all open tickets.


/tickets?priority=urgent

Retrieve all urgent tickets.


/tickets?category=payment

Retrieve all payment-related tickets.


/tickets?customer_name=Ali

Retrieve tickets belonging to customers whose name
matches "Ali".


Multiple filters:

/tickets?category=payment&priority=urgent&status=open

This should return only tickets that satisfy ALL of the
provided conditions.


4) Important Requirements

- Use FastAPI.
- Use Pydantic for request validation.
- Do not use a database.
- Store data in tickets.json.
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes.
- Handle cases where a ticket does not exist.
- Do not hardcode statistics.
- Keep JSON file reading/writing separate from the API endpoints.
- Test all endpoints using Swagger UI (/docs) or Postman.


